### How does non-sealed reopen the hierarchy?

Sealing restricts direct subtypes: the `permits` clause names exactly which types may directly implement the sealed interface. Choosing `non-sealed` on one of those permitted types is a deliberate statement that this branch is reopened.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, PartnerProduct {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}

final class CurrentAccount implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("250000"); }
}

// non-sealed: this branch is open for anyone
non-sealed class PartnerProduct implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
```

### Can unlisted classes extend the non-sealed branch?

Yes. Any class may extend `PartnerProduct` without being listed in any `permits` clause. The unlisted class becomes a subtype of `Account` transitively.

```java
package com.bank.partner;

import com.bank.model.PartnerProduct;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Not listed in any permits clause
public class WalletProduct extends PartnerProduct {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() {
        return new BigDecimal("15000");
    }
}

public class InsuranceProduct extends PartnerProduct {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() {
        return new BigDecimal("200000");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.partner.WalletProduct;

public class PartnerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account wallet = new WalletProduct();
        System.out.println("Wallet is Account: " + (wallet instanceof Account));
        System.out.println("Balance: " + wallet.balance() + " INR");
    }
}
```

```text
Wallet is Account: true
Balance: 15000 INR
```

### How does the sealing guarantee still hold?

The sealing guarantee is that only the permitted types directly implement the sealed interface. The unlisted class does not directly implement `Account`; it extends `PartnerProduct`, which is permitted. The first-level closure is intact.

```java
// Direct implementors of Account (sealed):
// ✓ SavingsAccount (final)
// ✓ CurrentAccount (final)
// ✓ PartnerProduct (non-sealed)

// WalletProduct extends PartnerProduct, NOT Account directly
// The sealed contract is not broken
```

### What is the impact on exhaustive switch?

Because the `non-sealed` branch is open, the compiler cannot enumerate the classes beneath it. But matching the `non-sealed` base as one case covers it and all its unknown subtypes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FeeCalculator {
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(Account account) {
        return switch (account) {
            case SavingsAccount s -> new BigDecimal("150.00");
            case CurrentAccount c -> new BigDecimal("300.00");
            case PartnerProduct p -> new BigDecimal("100.00");
            // PartnerProduct case covers WalletProduct,
            // InsuranceProduct, and any future partner products
            // No default needed: all permitted types are covered
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FeeCalculator calc = new FeeCalculator();
        System.out.println("Savings fee: " + calc.calculateFee(
                new SavingsAccount()));
        System.out.println("Wallet fee:  " + calc.calculateFee(
                new com.bank.partner.WalletProduct()));
    }
}
```

```text
Savings fee: 150.00
Wallet fee:  100.00
```

### When should you use non-sealed?

Use `non-sealed` for a plugin architecture: a closed core with one deliberately open extension point. The vendor controls the sealed set but opens exactly one branch for third-party implementations.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Closed core: final leaves
// Open extension point: non-sealed PartnerProduct
public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, PartnerProduct {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

// Core products: closed, cannot be extended
final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}
final class CurrentAccount implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("250000"); }
}

// Extension point: open for partners
non-sealed class PartnerProduct implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
```

### What are the trade-offs of non-sealed?

| Aspect | final/sealed branch | non-sealed branch |
|---|---|---|
| Extension | Restricted | Open |
| Exhaustive switch | Full leaf enumeration | Match base type only |
| Compiler guarantees | All subtypes known | Unknown subtypes possible |
| Use case | Core domain types | Plugin/extension points |

### What about testing with non-sealed branches?

Because the `non-sealed` branch is open, tests can create mock or stub implementations without modifying the sealed hierarchy.

```java
package com.bank.test;

import com.bank.model.PartnerProduct;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class MockPartnerProduct extends PartnerProduct {
    private final BigDecimal mockBalance;

    public MockPartnerProduct(BigDecimal mockBalance) {
        this.mockBalance = mockBalance;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() {
        return mockBalance;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Use `non-sealed` sparingly and deliberately. In a banking domain, keep the regulated core products `final` so the closed-set guarantee holds, and open only one branch for partners. This isolates the open extension point and makes it auditable rather than leaving a hole in the whole model. In exhaustive switches, always match the `non-sealed` base to handle unknown partner subtypes as a group.
